F.  e_s = 1 IS A STRUCTURAL IDENTITY OF THE BOUND, NOT A MEASUREMENT
============================================================================================
  dodge bound v = s/(tau + 2 sqrt(d/a)):  d ln v/d ln s = 1  (identically 1)
                                          d ln v/d ln a = sqrt(d)/(sqrt(a)*tau + 2*sqrt(d))
  stop bound:   d ln v/d ln s + d ln v/d ln a = 1
  Both hold for EVERY (s, a, tau) > 0, including values where the bound sits
  far above the speed the vehicle achieves.  An elasticity of 1 therefore
  carries no information about which limit binds; it is the statement that the
  bound is homogeneous of degree 1 in the range.

  The metric is the MINIMUM over active bounds.  Elasticity of min(v_perc, v_other):
   v_perc (m/s)  v_other (m/s)   d ln v_metric/d ln s
          16.92           7.00                   0.00
          12.00           7.00                   0.00
           6.00           7.00                   1.00
           6.90           7.00                   1.00
  With the measured 90-per-cent ceiling at <= 5-7 m/s and every perception bound
  computed above at 10-29 m/s, the metric's elasticity to range at the operating
  point is 0, not 1.  The simulator's own attempt to measure a range elasticity
  ON A COURSE (rather than on an isolated dodge) returned nan: every run failed
  at 5 m/s for an unrelated controller reason [simulator F13].

